{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788382878219,"sessionID":"ses_f9c130ecaffeXIzoGLIIVyKiu4","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8890fe213ab656d8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"gh issue view 152 2>&1 | head -100"},"output":"title:\tRuling: function signature ergonomics -- direction, not spelling\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\nlabels:\t\ncomments:\t1\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nissue-type:\t\nparent:\t\nsub-issues:\t\nsub-issues-completed:\t\nblocked-by:\t\nblocking:\t\nnumber:\t152\n--\nGrilled via docs/.grill/syntax-ergonomics.round.yaml, question function-signature-ergonomics.\n\n**Ruled: direction only, not a concrete spelling.** None of the four options as posed was picked outright. Maintainer's free-text response proposes a fifth shape that combines two of them: drop the idea of a special parameter name entirely -- `.` is always the input value of a function, the way it already is inside `map`/`select`. Toylang already has matching syntax; reuse it as the mechanism that simultaneously names the type and the destructuring rule, so the whole function signature becomes a matcher over its one input.\n\nMaintainer's exact words: \"i feel liek we can just say that there is no need for a special name fo thte parameter at all, just `.` is alwasy the input value of a function; but, we alreayd have matching sytax, eight? so, we can use the matching syntax as actually a way that simultaneously marks the type and the destructoriing rules; or basically the whole function becomes a matcher in a sense?\"\n\nThis is direction-only ratification (implicit-`.` + reuse-matching-syntax), per the standing rule that direction-only ratification precedes concrete-spelling grilling. Needs a follow-up round with real code examples of what a matcher-shaped `fn` signature looks like -- board row function-signature-matching-syntax tracks that.\n","metadata":{"output":"title:\tRuling: function signature ergonomics -- direction, not spelling\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\nlabels:\t\ncomments:\t1\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nissue-type:\t\nparent:\t\nsub-issues:\t\nsub-issues-completed:\t\nblocked-by:\t\nblocking:\t\nnumber:\t152\n--\nGrilled via docs/.grill/syntax-ergonomics.round.yaml, question function-signature-ergonomics.\n\n**Ruled: direction only, not a concrete spelling.** None of the four options as posed was picked outright. Maintainer's free-text response proposes a fifth shape that combines two of them: drop the idea of a special parameter name entirely -- `.` is always the input value of a function, the way it already is inside `map`/`select`. Toylang already has matching syntax; reuse it as the mechanism that simultaneously names the type and the destructuring rule, so the whole function signature becomes a matcher over its one input.\n\nMaintainer's exact words: \"i feel liek we can just say that there is no need for a special name fo thte parameter at all, just `.` is alwasy the input value of a function; but, we alreayd have matching sytax, eight? so, we can use the matching syntax as actually a way that simultaneously marks the type and the destructoriing rules; or basically the whole function becomes a matcher in a sense?\"\n\nThis is direction-only ratification (implicit-`.` + reuse-matching-syntax), per the standing rule that direction-only ratification precedes concrete-spelling grilling. Needs a follow-up round with real code examples of what a matcher-shaped `fn` signature looks like -- board row function-signature-matching-syntax tracks that.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"gh issue view 152 2>&1 | head -100","time":{"start":1788382878205,"end":1788382878216}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is the toylang project. Let me check the branch for the issue number: `issue-152`. Let me look at the issue via gh CLI, and also explore the codebase structure.\n\nThe task mentions a \"Board row\" and \"round-5 RULED: maintainer picked option A -- call-form hoist\". Let me look at the plans directory and check gh issue 152.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063ed13b9001aNG04EDdyE71Mx","sessionID":"ses_f9c130ecaffeXIzoGLIIVyKiu4","messageID":"msg_063ed033d0015c5pYYUsIFdMcz"}}
