{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788382912241,"sessionID":"ses_f9c130ecaffeXIzoGLIIVyKiu4","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9fcc1daac0281351","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"signature\\|matching-syntax\\|152\\|call-form\\|hoist\" plans/board.yaml"},"output":"7:- id: function-signature-matching-syntax\n8:  issue: gh:152\n9:  title: 'Round 5 RULED: maintainer picked A -- call-form hoist (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or Quit -> ...)), against the round-5 thesis which favored C on parse-today grounds. Maintainer note: \"we are building a new language and it''s in the early stages; most feature requests do not assume this will already parse and that is natural\" -- i.e. A''s three novel surfaces (new fn-declaration shape, return-type inference, type-name-as-match-call expression form) are accepted design cost, not disqualifying. Build task: implement A. gh:152 to be closed on landing.'\n345:  title: 'Grill the search vocabulary (choice, cut, .., ordering) and the applicative fold block -- unratified draft sections (draft-split). Round 1 leaned depth-first for `..` (Q7) but answer was \"also research the performance implications before we fully commit\"; round 2 needed that research before re-asking. Q7 was re-asked in the signature-matching-and-search-cut round and RULED provisional: `..` promises depth-first order (jq-compatible), flagged for reevaluation once recursive-descent-order-research lands. Round 4 answers (search-vocab-choice-cut-fold, no picks made): Q search-choice-point-syntax -- no pick, maintainer asks whether the choice-point operator should instead be spelled parallel to match syntax rather than a new infix operator; needs search-choice-point-match-syntax-research before re-ask. Q search-cut-semantics -- no pick, maintainer leans \"first should be a real prelude function\" (tentative, not ratified -- re-ask against that framing, i.e. option B reworded as an ordinary stdlib fn rather than compiler-magic sugar). Q applicative-fold-block-syntax -- no pick, maintainer questions whether fold is needed at all (\"fold is basically the same as reduce... reduce is usually too low level, a code smell, results in confusing code\"); before continuing fold syntax design, next round must first ask whether applicative-fold-block-syntax should be justified or dropped.'\n353:  title: 'Research spike (round answer, signature-matching-and-search-cut round, question recursive-descent-order): Q7 was ruled provisional -- `..` promises depth-first order (jq-compatible) -- but the maintainer wants a deep research brief on what alternatives actually exist to the unordered/columnar-fast-path design (questions.md Q7), so the ruling can be reevaluated later with real alternatives in hand rather than the two options already on the table. Survey how other query/traversal languages and engines (jq, XPath, JSONPath, columnar/vectorized query engines) handle recursive descent order versus set semantics, and what a toylang-specific unordered fast path would cost callers who need order back. Write findings to plans/.'\n376:  title: 'Implement `--explain-offload`: an opt-in compiler diagnostic that annotates compiled output with which sub-expressions became vectorized kernels and which fell back, and why -- per the offload-boundary-design round 2 ruling (multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question vectorizability-visibility, option B). No signature or type-system change; a build-time flag only. LSP hover (option C) is a wanted future addition, deliberately unscheduled here.'\n","metadata":{"output":"7:- id: function-signature-matching-syntax\n8:  issue: gh:152\n9:  title: 'Round 5 RULED: maintainer picked A -- call-form hoist (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or Quit -> ...)), against the round-5 thesis which favored C on parse-today grounds. Maintainer note: \"we are building a new language and it''s in the early stages; most feature requests do not assume this will already parse and that is natural\" -- i.e. A''s three novel surfaces (new fn-declaration shape, return-type inference, type-name-as-match-call expression form) are accepted design cost, not disqualifying. Build task: implement A. gh:152 to be closed on landing.'\n345:  title: 'Grill the search vocabulary (choice, cut, .., ordering) and the applicative fold block -- unratified draft sections (draft-split). Round 1 leaned depth-first for `..` (Q7) but answer was \"also research the performance implications before we fully commit\"; round 2 needed that research before re-asking. Q7 was re-asked in the signature-matching-and-search-cut round and RULED provisional: `..` promises depth-first order (jq-compatible), flagged for reevaluation once recursive-descent-order-research lands. Round 4 answers (search-vocab-choice-cut-fold, no picks made): Q search-choice-point-syntax -- no pick, maintainer asks whether the choice-point operator should instead be spelled parallel to match syntax rather than a new infix operator; needs search-choice-point-match-syntax-research before re-ask. Q search-cut-semantics -- no pick, maintainer leans \"first should be a real prelude function\" (tentative, not ratified -- re-ask against that framing, i.e. option B reworded as an ordinary stdlib fn rather than compiler-magic sugar). Q applicative-fold-block-syntax -- no pick, maintainer questions whether fold is needed at all (\"fold is basically the same as reduce... reduce is usually too low level, a code smell, results in confusing code\"); before continuing fold syntax design, next round must first ask whether applicative-fold-block-syntax should be justified or dropped.'\n353:  title: 'Research spike (round answer, signature-matching-and-search-cut round, question recursive-descent-order): Q7 was ruled provisional -- `..` promises depth-first order (jq-compatible) -- but the maintainer wants a deep research brief on what alternatives actually exist to the unordered/columnar-fast-path design (questions.md Q7), so the ruling can be reevaluated later with real alternatives in hand rather than the two options already on the table. Survey how other query/traversal languages and engines (jq, XPath, JSONPath, columnar/vectorized query engines) handle recursive descent order versus set semantics, and what a toylang-specific unordered fast path would cost callers who need order back. Write findings to plans/.'\n376:  title: 'Implement `--explain-offload`: an opt-in compiler diagnostic that annotates compiled output with which sub-expressions became vectorized kernels and which fell back, and why -- per the offload-boundary-design round 2 ruling (multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question vectorizability-visibility, option B). No signature or type-system change; a build-time flag only. LSP hover (option C) is a wanted future addition, deliberately unscheduled here.'\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"signature\\|matching-syntax\\|152\\|call-form\\|hoist\" plans/board.yaml","time":{"start":1788382912238,"end":1788382912240}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The board.yaml didn't have the match. Let me search more broadly for the board row.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063ed9d7f001R4Gi7QxW6YncWF","sessionID":"ses_f9c130ecaffeXIzoGLIIVyKiu4","messageID":"msg_063ed989c001fuk74jP77RAcvi"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788382913261,"sessionID":"ses_f9c130ecaffeXIzoGLIIVyKiu4","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-92b4b69e3056b35a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn \"signature-matching-syntax\\|call-form hoist\\|call-form\\|round-5\\|round 5\" plans/ docs/ research-log/ 2>/dev/null | head -30"},"output":"plans/board-archive.yaml:1351:  title: 'Research spike (round answer, signature-matching-and-search-cut round, question function-signature-matching-syntax): maintainer asked for deeper research on how each of the three candidate spellings (A: call-form hoist, B: anonymous param + bare match, C: additive `.`-binding) performs across a wider set of types and use cases a real language would face, not just the enum example probed so far -- structs/records, tuples, scalars, nested matches, recursive/self-referential types. Run each candidate''s shape against the real parser where possible (as round 3 did) and note where it breaks down or gets awkward. Write findings to plans/.'\nplans/board.yaml:7:- id: function-signature-matching-syntax\nplans/board.yaml:9:  title: 'Round 5 RULED: maintainer picked A -- call-form hoist (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or Quit -> ...)), against the round-5 thesis which favored C on parse-today grounds. Maintainer note: \"we are building a new language and it''s in the early stages; most feature requests do not assume this will already parse and that is natural\" -- i.e. A''s three novel surfaces (new fn-declaration shape, return-type inference, type-name-as-match-call expression form) are accepted design cost, not disqualifying. Build task: implement A. gh:152 to be closed on landing.'\nplans/incidents/issue-169-20260901/20260901-010814-issue-169.jsonl.tail:34:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788217712747,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-ad6d7ae8b4a63a12\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"complet