round 2 presents the maintainer''s own hoist-into-signature sketch (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or ...)) as a concrete candidate (gh:152). Round 2 answer: no pick yet; round 3 stress-tested each of the three candidates (A/B/C) against the real parser and re-asked. Round 3 answer: still no pick, needs signature-matching-deeper-research first; round 4 presented that research (all six type shapes) with full code probes per option. Round 4 answer: still no pick, and it is process feedback, not a content signal -- option cards were too long (\\\"at least 8 times shorter\\\"), code belongs once in the thesis not repeated per option, and rounds should frame how each option interacts with future plans rather than asking for an early commit. grill-via-annotations/SKILL.md''s option-card rule updated to match (2026-09-02). Round 5 must re-ask the same A/B/C content (A and B still fail identically pre-shape, C still the only candidate that parses all six shapes) in the new short-card, future-framed shape -- no new research needed.'\\n  kind: decide\\n  needs: [signature-matching-deeper-research]\\n  prio: 3\\n  status: todo\\n\\n- id: declare-terminator-build\\n  issue: gh:153\\n  title: Require an explicit terminator after fn/type declarations, retire the same-line heuristic (issue #9 oddity ruling)\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: []\\n  prio: 3\\n  status: delegated\\n\\n- id: dsv-partials-migration\\n  issue: gh:136\\n  title: 'Ruled provisional (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): csv/tsv stay compiler-predefined names until source_in_fn allows stream sources inside function bodies AND a partial-application mechanism is ratified+built -- then move both into prelude.toy as ordinary shadowable partials. Blocked-open, not a live task yet.'\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: [partial-application-system-design]\\n  prio: 5\\n  status: todo\\n\\n- id: euler-slow-fragments-2\\n  issue: gh:93\\n  title: 'Apply the slow-fragment tier to batch 3''s correct-but-slow problems (gh:90 ruling). Parked after 4 commitless automated runs -- maintainer ruling (2026-09-01): hand off to a privileged manual session rather than redispatch. Prompt drafted at plans/issue-93-privileged-agent-prompt.md. Lane stays escalated (~/.cache/toylang-drive/escalated-issue-93 kept) until that session lands it.'\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: [docs-slow-fragment-tier]\\n  prio: 3\\n  status: todo\\n\\n- id: variant-types-flip\\n  issue: gh:165\\n  title: 'Variants become capitalized types with lowercase constructors, per the auto-matchers ratification -- subtype rules planned first, then the migration (corpus, examples, README, and docs/guides+tutorial+reference). Lane issue-165 stalled here (0 commits, renamed aside as issue-165-contaminated-20260831): the brief claims checker enforcement \\\"already done via gh:156\\\" but variant-checker-capital-first was still status: todo -- added as a real needs edge below so the migration cannot be dispatched again until the checker actually enforces the rule.'\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: [matcher-totality-and-alt-design, variant-checker-capital-first]\\n  prio: 2\\n  model: fable\\n  status: todo\\n\\n- id: shell-out-build\\n  issue: gh:158\\n  title: Build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive for shell-out calls, e.g. lines | pipe_through(\\\"grep\\\", [...]) | collect (shell-out-design ruling, gh:158, depends on Q35's stdout/stderr effect model)\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: [stdout-stderr-effect-model-design]\\n  status: todo\\n\\n- id: stdout-stderr-effect-model-design\\n  title: 'Decide Q35 (plans/questions.md#q35): what are stdout/stderr, and does a program write or return? Two commitless runs on shell-out-build (gh:158, issue-158, 2026-09-01) both spent their full budget rediscovering that pipe_through cannot be implemented until this effect model exists, then produced no code. Round 1 answer: no pick -- maintainer''s freeText pulled two directions at once (\\\"well i would say real type, but isn''t it already the same types that we are using for our own program''s own inputs and outputs?\\\") and flagged a second open question riding along, how stdin/stdout splitting works. Needs stdout-stderr-effect-model-research (does the already-decided \\\"Stream is the effect layer\\\" model already cover a mid-pipeline write, or does it need a real Effect/IO type; how does stdin/stdout splitting fit) before a sharper round 2.'\\n  kind: decide\\n  needs: [stdout-stderr-effect-model-research]\\n  prio: 1\\n  status: todo\\n\\n- id: stdout-stderr-effect-model-research\\n  title: 'Research spike (round 1 answer, stdout-stderr-effect-model question): the maintainer''s freeText was torn between \\\"a real type\\\" (option C, Effect/IO) and noting the program already has Stream-typed inputs/outputs (option B, extend the decided \\\"Stream is the effect layer\\\" model) -- and separately flagged that stdin/stdout splitting still needs deciding. Write up how each of the three round-1 options (opaque plumbing, Stream-carries-writes, real Effect/IO type) would actually model reading and writing on two separate streams (stdout vs stderr) using types the language already has, so round 2 can present concrete, verified code for each rather than re-asking the same abstract question. Write findings to plans/.'\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: []\\n  prio: 1\\n  status: delegated\\n\\n- id: stdin-redesign-build-2\\n  issue: gh:172\\n  title: 'Re-file of gh:159 (maintainer cleanup ruling, 2026-09-02, option C: drop the poisoned issue-159 lane, re-board under a fresh lane id rather than repair it). Retire the three stdin keywords for one Stream<Str> source, parse(.) as a library call (stdin-syntax-design ruling, tentative -- verify parse(.) resolves an expected type inside a map body before full build). The old issue-159 branch/worktree are abandoned in place (permission-denied cleanup, left as garbage per the ruling); the feature diff there (commit 45c76be) is reference-only, re-derive against current main rather than cherry-pick.'\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: []\\n  model: fable\\n  status: delegated\\n\\n- id: trait-interface-build\\n  title: 'Ruled (trait-interface-design round): (1) Rust-shaped declaration -- trait Name { fn sig(...) -> T } / impl Name for Type { fn sig(...) -> T = body }; (2) builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) tentatively become source-written prelude impls \\\"when practical, otherwise built in\\\" -- maintainer explicitly declined to fully commit either way (\\\"in some cases performance may decide this... we would need to see how it works out in practice\\\"), so start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism) and fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance; revisit once build experience exists; (3) named trait methods are colon-only -- x:foo(y) is the only spelling, no plain-call fallback even when unambiguous.'\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: []\\n  status: todo\\n\\n- id: mutation-semantics-spike\\n  title: 'Ruled (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): maintainer leans privileged/shared-reference mutation-as-optimization but will not ratify without prototyping -- spike the \\\"provably one reference exists\\\" static analysis against a couple of backends before a real decide row reopens this'\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: []\\n  prio: 4\\n  status: todo\\n\\n- id: float-build-rust\\n  issue: gh:149\\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Rust backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: [float-build]\\n  status: delegated\\n\\n- id: float-build-python\\n  issue: gh:149\\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Python backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: [float-build]\\n  status: delegated\\n\\n- id: float-build-go\\n  issue: gh:149\\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Go backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: [float-build]\\n  status: delegated\\n\\n- id: float-build-lua\\n  issue: gh:149\\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Lua backend only (host hook), following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: [float-build]\\n  status: todo\\n\\n- id: float-build-jq\\n  issue: gh:149\\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the jq backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: [float-build]\\n  status: todo\\n\\n- id: float-build-c-escalation\\n  issue: gh:149\\n  title: Attempt Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the C/LLVM backend; if there is no usable native matching primitive, write ESCALATION.md scoped to just this backend, commit it, and stop rather than stalling.\\n  kind: build\\n  needs: [float-build]\\n  status: todo\\n\\n- id: benchmark-suite-build\\n  title: Build the initial benchmark suite -- 8 non-float CLBG tasks, hyperfine harness per benchmark-plan.md, framed as a general cross-language competitiveness claim (gh:147 ruling); n-body/spectral-norm deferred to a second wave (gh:146 ruling)\\n  kind: build\",\"lineStart\":1,\"lineEnd\":140,\"totalLines\":431,\"truncated\":true}},\"title\":\"plans/board.yaml\",\"time\":{\"start\":1788379841189,\"end\":1788379841200}},\"metadata\":{\"openrouter\":{\"reasoning_details\":[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Let me look at the board.yaml row to see the state and dependencies.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]}},\"id\":\"prt_063bebe42001sP41x0M1081VgN\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw\",\"messageID\":\"msg_063beb5d2001JXgYTjTNa4KonK\"}}\nplans/signature-matching-deeper-research.md:36:## Candidate A: call-form hoist\nplans/signature-matching-deeper-research.md:38:`fn area = Shape(circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0)` -- the function is a call-form match:type\nplans/signature-matching-deeper-research.md:60:**The call-form head means nothing as an expression either.** `a_inner` keeps a normal signature\nplans/signature-matching-deeper-research.md:61:and writes the call-form as a body expression, to test the head independently of the fn-grammar\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rn \"signature-matching-syntax\\|call-form hoist\\|call-form\\|round-5\\|round 5\" plans/ docs/ research-log/ 2>/dev/null | head -30","time":{"start":1788382913255,"end":1788382913257}},"id":"prt_063ed9eea001ZY4MfZ3OFcI1p5","sessionID":"ses_f9c130ecaffeXIzoGLIIVyKiu4","messageID":"msg_063ed989c001fuk74jP77RAcvi"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788382913280,"sessionID":"ses_f9c130ecaffeXIzoGLIIVyKiu4","part":{"id":"prt_063eda2f9001cCxm5iIP7TGu1w","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"8bf6152e0cd881acdd34aa0966f384f7ae13fecc","messageID":"msg_063ed989c001fuk74jP77RAcvi","sessionID":"ses_f9c130ecaffeXIzoGLIIVyKiu4","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":20796,"input":925,"output":138,"reasoning":21,"cache":{"write":0,"read":19712}},"cost":0.000404137}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788382914601,"sessionID":"ses_f9c130ecaffeXIzoGLIIVyKiu4","part":{"id":"prt_063eda822001io11mZ27qz4WSx","messageID":"msg_063eda315001I0T9PURszcC135","sessionID":"ses_f9c130ecaffeXIzoGLIIVyKiu4","snapshot":"8bf6152e0cd881acdd34aa0966f384f7ae13fecc","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788382916837,"sessionID":"ses_f9c130ecaffeXIzoGLIIVyKiu4","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8263a317460d1626","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-152/plans/signature-matching-deeper-research.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-152/plans/signature-matching-deeper-research.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Function-signature matching syntax: how the three spellings hold up across type shapes\n2: \n3: The deeper-research legwork for gh:152 (round answer, signature-matching-and-search-cut\n4: round).. Round 3 stress-tested the three candidate spellings against the real parser on the enum\n5: example and re-asked;the maintainer wanted the same across structs/records, tuples, scalars,\n6: nested matches, and recursive/self-referential types. This run probed each spelling over every\n7: shape the language actually has, and the verdicts below are what the real parser and checker said.\n8: \n9: The probe programs live in `scratch/`, run through `toylang::fmt` + `toylang::compile` (parse\n10: and check only, no codegen was attempted),both untracked in this worktree. One shape in the required list does not exist: **there is no tuple\n11: type.** The language's product type is the record (`{x: Int, y: Int}`),and the record probes\n12: below are what stand in for the tuple dimension. Probing an anonymous tuple would mean inventing a\n13: type form first, which is a different question from the spelling one. \n14: \n15: ## The baseline: every shape compiles today\n16: \n17: All six baseline programs -- enum, record, scalar, vec, nested, recursive -- parse and check\n18: clean. Each is a named param plus an explicit `|` subject;the subject is spelled out everywhere\n19: the language currently lets you match:\n20: \n21: ```toylang\n22: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n23: fn area(s: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0\n24: \n25: area(Shape.point)\n26: ```\n27: \n28: The record one destructures through guard projections (`p |.x > .y -> .x or .y`),the vec\n29: through `select`, the nested through a payload arm then a second match, and the recursive through a\n30: `sum` over a `map` that calls the function again. All compile as written. They are the floor the\n31: three spellings are measured against. A and B never reach type shape: their failures fire at\n32: parse and at the subject check, before any shape-dependent reasoning, so their probes are mostly\n33: enum-shaped. C is the only spelling whose probes parse as written, so it is the one the shape sweep\n34: actually differentiates. \n35: \n36: ## Candidate A: call-form hoist\n37: \n38: `fn area = Shape(circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0)` -- the function is a call-form match:type\n39: name as head, arms as arguments, no param list, no return annotation. The maintainer's sketch\n40: was `fn render = Msg(Ping -> \"*ping*\" or Quit -> \"*quit*\" or Text -> .body)`. \n41: \n42: **The signature shape is not parseable.** The fn grammar requires\n43: `fn name(param: Type) -> Type = body` (parse.rs:585-622:the parens, the param name,\n44: the return annotation, each mandatory). Every A probe dies at the same byte:\n45: \n46: ```toylang\n47: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n48: fn area = Shape(circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0)\n49: \n50: area(Shape.point)\n51: ```\n52: \n53: `a_enum`, `a_nested` (`Wrapped(w -> Shape(circle{r} -> ... or ...))` as head),and `a_vec`\n54: (`Vec<Int>(any() -> . | select(. >= 2))` as head) all die identically: \"expected `(`, found\n55: `=`\". The shape being matched -- enum, nested enum, vec -- never reaches the parser;the wall\n56: is the fn declaration shape itself. So A is not a spelling of the existing fn signature;it is a\n57: new fn-declaration form. And the maintainer sketch omits the return type too, so the form also\n58: needs return-type inference (or a return position A does not spell)erto work.\n59: \n60: **The call-form head means nothing as an expression either.** `a_inner` keeps a normal signature\n61: and writes the call-form as a body expression, to test the head independently of the fn-grammar\n62: question:\n63: \n64: ```toylang\n65: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n66: fn area(s: Shape) -> Int = Shape(circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0)\n67: \n68: area(Shape.point)\n69: ```\n70: \n71: `a_inner` parses,and the checker answers \"`Shape` is not a function\". Enum construction is\n72: `Shape.circle` or the bare `circle{r: 1}` (enums.md),not `Shape(...)`;the application path\n73: only resolves functions and variant constructors (check/mod.rs:1883-1891). So A's head is a new\n74: expression form -- \"a type name used as a match-call\" -- not a reuse of anything. For the vec\n75: shape the head would additionally be a generic type-name call (`Vec<Int>(...)`),even further from any\n76: existing application, but it never gets far enough to test that. A's nested probe was written to\n77: see whether arms compose head-in-head, and it dies at the same grammar wall, so nesting behavior\n78: under A is unobserved. Record, scalar, and tuple-shaped heads would hit the same two walls:the\n79: head is uniformly a type name, and both failures fire before type resolution. Nothing shape-\n80: specific differentiates A.\n81: \n82: ## Candidate B: anonymous param + bare match\n83: \n84: Two probes isolate the two halves, because each half fails on its own:\n85: \n86: **Anonymous `: Type` is not parseable.**\n87: \n88: ```toylang\n89: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n90: fn area(: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0\n91: \n92: area(Shape.point)\n93: ```\n94: \n95: `b_param` (and `b_recursive`) die at the param: \"expected a name, found `:`\" (parse.rs:594).\n96: The fn grammar demands an identifier before the colon. Dropping the name is a parse-level grammar\n97: change, independent of everything downstream. \n98: \n99: **A bare match body has no subject.**\n100: \n101: ```toylang\n102: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n103: fn area(s: Shape) -> Int = circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0\n104: \n105: area(Shape.point)\n106: ```\n107: \n108: `b_body` parses fine,and the checker answers \"a match needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\"\n109: (check/mod.rs:1300). A match chain's subject arrives through a pipe -- each arm body stops at `|`\n110: and `or`, which is what lets the chain be one pipe stage (match.md:3; parse.rs:898). A bare body\n111: presents no pipe stage, so the match has no subject to check against. B needs the checker to accept\n112: a subject-less match at body top and treat the input as that subject -- the same body-top-binding idea\n113: C needs, but without spelling it. The recursive case (`b_recursive_body`, `num -> . or arr ->\n114: sum(...)`) hits the identical wall;recursion adds nothing shape-specific for B either. Both B\n115: walls fire before the subject's type is examined, so record, scalar, vec, and nested shapes\n116: would fail identically;no further probes were needed there. \n117: \n118: The bare-subject philosophy already has a precedent against it: **the language refuses bare function\n119: calls in pipe stages.** `xs | sum` errors \"`sum` is a function, not a value; write `sum(...)`\n120: to call it\", while `xs | sum(.)` compiles (`probe_sum_bare` vs `probe_sum_parens`). The subject\n121: is explicit everywhere a value enters a stage;a bare match would be the first subject-less\n122: construct. \n123: \n124: ## Candidate C: additive `.`-binding\n125: \n126: ```toylang\n127: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n128: fn area(s: Shape) -> Int = . | circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0\n129: \n130: area(Shape.point)\n131: ```\n132: \n133: `. ` rebinds at each boundary that introduces a subject: a pipe stage, a `map` or `select` body,\n134: a match arm over a bare payload (pipe.md:29). C's proposal is one more boundary:the fn body top\n135: binds `.` to the input param, so the whole function is the match chain over its one input,with the\n136: signature unchanged. \n137: \n138: All six C probes -- enum, record, scalar, vec, nested, recursive -- parse clean;every one\n139: fails at the same single check: \"`.` is not bound here\" at the body's first `.` (check/mod.rs:1507).\n140: The fn body top is not currently a `.`-binding boundary, so `Expr::Subject` finds no subject. Each\n141: C program is the corresponding baseline program with exactly the `s |` / `p |` / `n |` / `xs |` /\n142: `x |` / `j |` prefix replaced by `. |`;nothing downstream differs, and every baseline compiles.\n143: \n144: So C is one additive change -- bind `.` to the input at body top -- with no grammar surface,and no\n145: shape-specific interaction in any of the probed shapes. The inner `.`-uses (match arms, `select`,\n146: `map`, `sum(.)`) already work today;each probed body's remainder is the compiling baseline's, so\n147: nothing downstream of the leading `.` remains to fail once the binding exists. \n148: \n149: **The one wrinkle found: recursion wants the reduce inside the arm.** `probe_rec_explicit` moved the\n150: recursion out of the arm chain, keeping the explicit `j |` subject so the `.`-uses inside lambdas\n151: could be tested independently of C's top-level binding:\n152: \n153: ```toylang\n154: enum Json { arr(Vec<Json>), num(Int) }\n155: fn total(j: Json) -> Int = j | num -> . or arr -> . | map(. | total(.)) | sum(.)\n156: ```\n157: \n158: It fails type-checking: \"expected Int, found Vec<Json>\" at the `arr` arm's body `.` -- match arms\n159: each get checked against the declared `-> Int`, so an arm may not hand the payload onward for a later\n160: stage to reduce. The recursion must stay inside the arm (`arr -> sum(. | map(. | total(.)))`),\n161: exactly as the baseline spells it. That constraint applies to whichever spelling wins -- B's\n162: bare-match recursion dies one step earlier at the subject wall,and C's recursion has nothing else\n163: between it and the baseline. \n164: \n165: ## What each candidate would cost\n166: \n167: - **A**: a new fn-declaration shape (or optional parens and return annotation), return-type\n168:   inference, and a type-name-as-match-call expression form. Three novel surfaces, all hit before\n169:   type shape can differentiate anything.\n170: - **B**: anonymous params (parse grammar) plus subject-less match chains at body top (checker\n171:   rule). Two independent changes;neither shape-specific;and the bare subject fights the language's\n172:   explicitness convention.\n173: \n174: - **C**: one additive boundary in `.`-rebinding. No grammar change;no shape-specific interaction\n175:   found;and it is the only spelling whose probed programs parse as written today.\n\n(End of file - total 175 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Function-signature matching syntax: how the three spellings hold up across type shapes\n\nThe deeper-research legwork for gh:152 (round answer, signature-matching-and-search-cut\nround).. Round 3 stress-tested the three candidate spellings against the real parser on the enum\nexample and re-asked;the maintainer wanted the same across structs/records, tuples, scalars,\nnested matches, and recursive/self-referential types. This run probed each spelling over every\nshape the language actually has, and the verdicts below are what the real parser and checker said.\n\nThe probe programs live in `scratch/`, run through `toylang::fmt` + `toylang::compile` (parse\nand check only, no codegen was attempted),both untracked in this worktree. One shape in the required list does not exist: **there is no tuple\ntype.** The language's product type is the record (`{x: Int, y: Int}`),and the record probes\nbelow are what stand in for the tuple dimension. Probing an anonymous tuple would mean inventing a\ntype form first, which is a different question from the spelling one. \n\n## The baseline: every shape compiles today\n\nAll six baseline programs -- enum, record, scalar, vec, nested, recursive -- parse and check\nclean. Each is a named param plus an explicit `|` subject;the subject is spelled out everywhere\nthe language currently lets you match:\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-152/plans/signature-matching-deeper-research.md","text":"# Function-signature matching syntax: how the three spellings hold up across type shapes\n\nThe deeper-research legwork for gh:152 (round answer, signature-matching-and-search-cut\nround).. Round 3 stress-tested the three candidate spellings against the real parser on the enum\nexample and re-asked;the maintainer wanted the same across structs/records, tuples, scalars,\nnested matches, and recursive/self-referential types. This run probed each spelling over every\nshape the language actually has, and the verdicts below are what the real parser and checker said.\n\nThe probe programs live in `scratch/`, run through `toylang::fmt` + `toylang::compile` (parse\nand check only, no codegen was attempted),both untracked in this worktree. One shape in the required list does not exist: **there is no tuple\ntype.** The language's product type is the record (`{x: Int, y: Int}`),and the record probes\nbelow are what stand in for the tuple dimension. Probing an anonymous tuple would mean inventing a\ntype form first, which is a different question from the spelling one. \n\n## The baseline: every shape compiles today\n\nAll six baseline programs -- enum, record, scalar, vec, nested, recursive -- parse and check\nclean. Each is a named param plus an explicit `|` subject;the subject is spelled out everywhere\nthe language currently lets you match:\n\n```toylang\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\nfn area(s: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0\n\narea(Shape.point)\n```\n\nThe record one destructures through guard projections (`p |.x > .y -> .x or .y`),the vec\nthrough `select`, the nested through a payload arm then a second match, and the recursive through a\n`sum` over a `map` that calls the function again. All compile as written. They are the floor the\nthree spellings are measured against. A and B never reach type shape: their failures fire at\nparse and at the subject check, before any shape-dependent reasoning, so their probes are mostly\nenum-shaped. C is the only spelling whose probes parse as written, so it is the one the shape sweep\nactually differentiates. \n\n## Candidate A: call-form hoist\n\n`fn area = Shape(circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0)` -- the function is a call-form match:type\nname as head, arms as arguments, no param list, no return annotation. The maintainer's sketch\nwas `fn render = Msg(Ping -> \"*ping*\" or Quit -> \"*quit*\" or Text -> .body)`. \n\n**The signature shape is not parseable.** The fn grammar requires\n`fn name(param: Type) -> Type = body` (parse.rs:585-622:the parens, the param name,\nthe return annotation, each mandatory). Every A probe dies at the same byte:\n\n```toylang\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\nfn area = Shape(circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0)\n\narea(Shape.point)\n```\n\n`a_enum`, `a_nested` (`Wrapped(w -> Shape(circle{r} -> ... or ...))` as head),and `a_vec`\n(`Vec<Int>(any() -> . | select(. >= 2))` as head) all die identically: \"expected `(`, found\n`=`\". The shape being matched -- enum, nested enum, vec -- never reaches the parser;the wall\nis the fn declaration shape itself. So A is not a spelling of the existing fn signature;it is a\nnew fn-declaration form. And the maintainer sketch omits the return type too, so the form also\nneeds return-type inference (or a return position A does not spell)erto work.\n\n**The call-form head means nothing as an expression either.** `a_inner` keeps a normal signature\nand writes the call-form as a body expression, to test the head independently of the fn-grammar\nquestion:\n\n```toylang\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\nfn area(s: Shape) -> Int = Shape(circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0)\n\narea(Shape.point)\n```\n\n`a_inner` parses,and the checker answers \"`Shape` is not a function\". Enum construction is\n`Shape.circle` or the bare `circle{r: 1}` (enums.md),not `Shape(...)`;the application path\nonly resolves functions and variant constructors (check/mod.rs:1883-1891). So A's head is a new\nexpression form -- \"a type name used as a match-call\" -- not a reuse of anything. For the vec\nshape the head would additionally be a generic type-name call (`Vec<Int>(...)`),even further from any\nexisting application, but it never gets far enough to test that. A's nested probe was written to\nsee whether arms compose head-in-head, and it dies at the same grammar wall, so nesting behavior\nunder A is unobserved. Record, scalar, and tuple-shaped heads would hit the same two walls:the\nhead is uniformly a type name, and both failures fire before type resolution. Nothing shape-\nspecific differentiates A.\n\n## Candidate B: anonymous param + bare match\n\nTwo probes isolate the two halves, because each half fails on its own:\n\n**Anonymous `: Type` is not parseable.**\n\n```toylang\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\nfn area(: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0\n\narea(Shape.point)\n```\n\n`b_param` (and `b_recursive`) die at the param: \"expected a name, found `:`\" (parse.rs:594).\nThe fn grammar demands an identifier before the colon. Dropping the name is a parse-level grammar\nchange, independent of everything downstream. \n\n**A bare match body has no subject.**\n\n```toylang\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\nfn area(s: Shape) -> Int = circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0\n\narea(Shape.point)\n```\n\n`b_body` parses fine,and the checker answers \"a match needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\"\n(check/mod.rs:1300). A match chain's subject arrives through a pipe -- each arm body stops at `|`\nand `or`, which is what lets the chain be one pipe stage (match.md:3; parse.rs:898). A bare body\npresents no pipe stage, so the match has no subject to check against. B needs the checker to accept\na subject-less match at body top and treat the input as that subject -- the same body-top-binding idea\nC needs, but without spelling it. The recursive case (`b_recursive_body`, `num -> . or arr ->\nsum(...)`) hits the identical wall;recursion adds nothing shape-specific for B either. Both B\nwalls fire before the subject's type is examined, so record, scalar, vec, and nested shapes\nwould fail identically;no further probes were needed there. \n\nThe bare-subject philosophy already has a precedent against it: **the language refuses bare function\ncalls in pipe stages.** `xs | sum` errors \"`sum` is a function, not a value; write `sum(...)`\nto call it\", while `xs | sum(.)` compiles (`probe_sum_bare` vs `probe_sum_parens`). The subject\nis explicit everywhere a value enters a stage;a bare match would be the first subject-less\nconstruct. \n\n## Candidate C: additive `.`-binding\n\n```toylang\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\nfn area(s: Shape) -> Int = . | circle{r} -> r * r or point ->  0\n\narea(Shape.point)\n```\n\n`. ` rebinds at each boundary that introduces a subject: a pipe stage, a `map` or `select` body,\na match arm over a bare payload (pipe.md:29). C's proposal is one more boundary:the fn body top\nbinds `.` to the input param, so the whole function is the match chain over its one input,with the\nsignature unchanged. \n\nAll six C probes -- enum, record, scalar, vec, nested, recursive -- parse clean;every one\nfails at the same single check: \"`.` is not bound here\" at the body's first `.` (check/mod.rs:1507).\nThe fn body top is not currently a `.`-binding boundary, so `Expr::Subject` finds no subject. Each\nC program is the corresponding baseline program with exactly the `s |` / `p |` / `n |` / `xs |` /\n`x |` / `j |` prefix replaced by `. |`;nothing downstream differs, and every baseline compiles.\n\nSo C is one additive change -- bind `.` to the input at body top -- with no grammar surface,and no\nshape-specific interaction in any of the probed shapes. The inner `.`-uses (match arms, `select`,\n`map`, `sum(.)`) already work today;each probed body's remainder is the compiling baseline's, so\nnothing downstream of the leading `.` remains to fail once the binding exists. \n\n**The one wrinkle found: recursion wants the reduce inside the arm.** `probe_rec_explicit` moved the\nrecursion out of the arm chain, keeping the explicit `j |` subject so the `.`-uses inside lambdas\ncould be tested independently of C's top-level binding:\n\n```toylang\nenum Json { arr(Vec<Json>), num(Int) }\nfn total(j: Json) -> Int = j | num -> . or arr -> . | map(. | total(.)) | sum(.)\n```\n\nIt fails type-checking: \"expected Int, found Vec<Json>\" at the `arr` arm's body `.` -- match arms\neach get checked against the declared `-> Int`, so an arm may not hand the payload onward for a later\nstage to reduce. The recursion must stay inside the arm (`arr -> sum(. | map(. | total(.)))`),\nexactly as the baseline spells it. That constraint applies to whichever spelling wins -- B's\nbare-match recursion dies one step earlier at the subject wall,and C's recursion has nothing else\nbetween it and the baseline. \n\n## What each candidate would cost\n\n- **A**: a new fn-declaration shape (or optional parens and return annotation), return-type\n  inference, and a type-name-as-match-call expression form. Three novel surfaces, all hit before\n  type shape can differentiate anything.\n- **B**: anonymous params (parse grammar) plus subject-less match chains at body top (checker\n  rule). Two independent changes;neither shape-specific;and the bare subject fights the language's\n  explicitness convention.\n\n- **C**: one additive boundary in `.`-rebinding. No grammar change;no shape-specific interaction\n  found;and it is the only spelling whose probed programs parse as written today.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":175,"totalLines":175,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/signature-matching-deeper-research.md","time":{"start":1788382916815,"end":1788382916835}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is a substantial task. Let me read the deeper research document to understand what option A means, then dig into the parser.\n\nLet me read the research doc and understand the current language (parser, AST, types, matching).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063edaa33001UgfrUB01Ea8mi7","sessionID":"ses_f9c130ecaffeXIzoGLIIVyKiu4","messageID":"msg_063eda315001I0T9PURszcC135"}}
